  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:FSWl57UR4k0",
    "purpose": "A large ranking roundup of the best t-shirts Michael has tested across past videos plus newcomers. The shirts were measured, washed, stress-washed and checked for shrinkage and torque, then sorted into five categories, with a personal winner picked in each.",
    "video_type": "ranking",
    "thesis": "Almost anyone can make a good t-shirt today, so the 'best' one depends on personal preference. The video describes how each top shirt feels, fits and holds up so viewers can match one to what they want, and names a personal favourite per category.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Shirts were measured, washed per care instructions, stress-washed, then re-measured for shrinkage and torque.",
      "Five categories: all-arounders, Japan's might, heaviest monsters, supremes, and best value deals.",
      "Category picks: J.Press x American Trench (all-arounders), Wonder Looper (Japan), American Giant Everest (heavyweights), Merz b. Schwanen 2M15 (supremes), Front General Store or House of Blanks (value).",
      "Loopwheel knitting is explained as helping a shirt recover its shape better.",
      "Shirts on the 3Sixteen/House of Blanks fabric from a Canadian mill showed essentially zero torque.",
      "Extra shirts cut from the video are listed with shrink and torque data in the description.",
      "Video includes a Koio sneaker sponsor read."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction and method",
        "summary": "Shows torque on an 'old faithful' tee and explains the testing method (measuring, washing, stress-washing, torque). Lays out the five categories.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "The all-arounders",
        "summary": "Mid-to-high tier shirts recommendable to anyone: J.Press x American Trench, Buck Mason ToughKnit (possible Supima, heavy torque) and the 3Sixteen heavyweight two-pack (no torque, Canadian-made fabric).",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Koio sponsor read",
        "summary": "Sponsor segment for Koio sneakers, made in Italy, and the Capri model.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Japan's might",
        "summary": "Japanese tees: Iron Heart loopwheel, Warehouse slub-yarn tee, Wonder Looper ELS heather tee (zero torque), Samurai (cropped, size up) and Allevol as an alternative fit.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Heavyweight monsters",
        "summary": "The heaviest shirts: The Real McCoys Buco, The Flat Head, and the more wearable American Giant Everest Tee.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "The supremes",
        "summary": "Elevated, clean minimalist tees: Merz b. Schwanen 2M15 (updated version of the 215 from The Bear), Asket, Lady White Our T-Shirt, and Knickerbocker's discounted basics program.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 17
      },
      {
        "title": "Best budget deals",
        "summary": "Value picks: Uniqlo ($20), Muji (longer and wider), Front General Store ($40, made in Japan) and House of Blanks ($35, made in Canada, zero torque).",
        "first_segment": 18,
        "last_segment": 20
      },
      {
        "title": "Winner's circle and conclusion",
        "summary": "Mentions Bronson as an omission and argues the best tee is about preference. Picks a personal favourite in each category and points viewers to extra shirts in the description.",
        "first_segment": 20,
        "last_segment": 24
      }
    ],
